rally  in prison  the base camp looked like the yard there  was nothing glamorous about this set  look at that cell phone business bruh i  ain't got no cell phone business i don't  know what you're talking about  i must have heard wrong man  come on freddy i told you i know what  you're talking about that one cell phone  even the route i would take to get to  work  i took was the same route that i would  take to go see my nephew  dominic dupont when he was incarcerated  he's home now but  it was doing the filming of this that i  got a glimpse into  what his life must be like and what his  life was like for 20 years  it made me really sad that you know  there are people  in our society that are forced to live  like this i'm not saying that you  shouldn't be made to pay for your  mistakes  but when is when is enough enough and  when is a a person  deserving of a second chance and we got  to look at all the things that go wrong  with our justice system  in the night of and knowing that i have  family members that have fallen prey to  this  it was discouraging  leaving a character like freddie knight  on the night oven  and going to ken jones on when we rise  was a huge shift in the opposite  direction  so you know freddie he was he was dark  he was he was he had a lot of issues he  was tormented  it was a lot of potential lost with  freddie freddie could have been a lot of  things  and then here i go to to ken jones  who in a lot of ways ken jones was  an american hero i mean he had survived  so many wars he was on you know on the  front lines  of what it was like to be black in the  navy in the  late 60s early 70s and the amount of  racism he must have dealt with  then also having to hide the fact that  he was  secretly in love with uh another white  soldier  and the trauma of having this man his  love of his life meet his demise because  of him something he  he had done so by the time he leaves the  navy he has all this trauma he survived  so much  only to be put bac